    "result_text": "The full build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented per-country validation of the E.164 telephone's national-number length in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **New `E164CountryLength.java`** (`rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility class holding the per-country rule: `+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10. It exposes `isNationalLengthValid(e164)`, splits the country code from the national digits via longest-code-first matching (so prefix codes resolve correctly), and leaves any unlisted country code unconstrained beyond the generic bound.\n\n2. **`NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`** \u2014 now also rejects when the national length is wrong for the country, reusing the existing 400 `InvalidTelephoneException` rather than adding a parallel exception/handler.\n\n3. **`InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 extended the 400 message to describe the country-length requirement.\n\n**Design choices:**\n- I deliberately **left `TelephoneNormalizer.toE164` lenient** (generic 8\u201315 digits). It's reused by `EnsureUniqueTelephone` to canonicalize *stored* owners for duplicate comparison; tightening it there would make legacy seed numbers (e.g. `6085551023`) silently drop out of the uniqueness check. The stricter country-length rule belongs only on the create-request validation path, so it lives in its own unit invoked from the normalize step.\n- The per-country length knowledge sits in one cohesive table, not scattered or grafted onto an existing class.\n\n**Verification:** `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the full `./mvnw test` build is green.",
